204
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:31,040
Last time we talked about the history of television.

205
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:36,280
Today, I'd like to talk about the roles television has been playing in Americans'

206
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:42,120
political life. Television has transformed politics in the United States by changing

207
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:47,500
the way in information is spread, by altering political campaigns, and by changing

208
00:19:47,500 --> 00:19:50,120
citizens' patterns of response to politics.

209
00:19:50,720 --> 00:19:55,620
By giving citizens independent access to the candidates, television diminished the

210
00:19:55,620 --> 00:19:59,720
role of the political party in the selection of the major party candidates.

211
00:19:59,720 --> 00:20:05,080
By centering politics on the person of the candidate, television accelerated the

212
00:20:05,080 --> 00:20:08,020
citizens' focus on character rather than issues.

213
00:20:08,760 --> 00:20:12,520
Television has altered the forms of political communication as well.

214
00:20:13,080 --> 00:20:17,040
The messages on which most of us rely are briefer than they once were.

215
00:20:17,740 --> 00:20:22,200
The stump speech, a political speech given by traveling politicians and lasting

216
00:20:22,200 --> 00:20:28,440
hours, which characterized 19th century political discourse, has given way to the 30

217
00:20:28,440 --> 00:20:32,880
-second advertisement and the 10 -second sound bite in broadcast news.

218
00:20:33,460 --> 00:20:37,300
Increasingly, the audience for speeches is not that standing in front of the

219
00:20:37,300 --> 00:20:41,980
politician, but rather the viewing audience who will hear and see a part of the

220
00:20:41,980 --> 00:20:43,280
speech on the news.

221
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:48,600
In these abbreviated forms, much of what constituted the traditional political

222
00:20:48,600 --> 00:20:51,500
discourse of earlier ages has been lost.

223
00:20:51,500 --> 00:20:57,820
In 15 or 30 seconds, a speaker cannot establish the historical context that shaped

224
00:20:57,820 --> 00:21:02,840
the issue in question, cannot detail the probable causes of the problem, and cannot

225
00:21:02,840 --> 00:21:06,940
examine alternative proposals to argue that one is preferable to others.

226
00:21:07,620 --> 00:21:10,220
Politicians assert, but do not argue.

227
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:15,720
Because television is an intimate medium, speaking through it requires a changed

228
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:21,640
political style that is more conversational, personal, and visual than that of the

229
00:21:21,640 --> 00:21:23,500
old style stump speech.

230
00:21:24,340 --> 00:21:28,560
Reliance on television means that increasingly, our political world contains

231
00:21:28,560 --> 00:21:31,600
memorable pictures rather than memorable words.

232
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:34,920
Schools teach us to analyze words and print.

233
00:21:35,440 --> 00:21:40,760
However, in a world in which politics is increasingly visual, informed citizenship

234
00:21:40,760 --> 00:21:42,820
requires a new set of skills.

235
00:21:43,540 --> 00:21:49,280
Recognizing the power of television's pictures, politicians craft televisual staged

236
00:21:49,280 --> 00:21:54,340
events called pseudo -event designed to attract media coverage.

237
00:21:54,880 --> 00:21:59,780
of the political activity we see on television news has been crafted by politicians,

